co Police Department's Extensive and Prolonged Investigation\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The San Francisco Police Department (SFPD) played a significant and prolonged role in investigating the Zodiac Killer. The department actively pursued the case, receiving public tips and holding press conferences to update the public [Data: Entities (221)]. Inspector David Toschi, a notable figure, was employed by the SFPD during his involvement in the Zodiac investigation [Data: Entities (221, 419)]. The Homicide Unit, a part of the S.F.P.D., was specifically responsible for investigating murders, including the high-profile Zodiac case [Data: Entities (222, 559); Relationships (1411)]. The Zodiac investigation was officially deactivated by the San Francisco Police Department in 2004, though related cases in other counties reportedly remain open [Data: Entities (659, 221); Relationships (1672)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"David Toschi's Central Role and Career-Altering Accusations\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Inspector David Toschi was a prominent detective with the San Francisco Police Department, known for his deep involvement in investigating both the Zodiac and Zebra Murders [Data: Entities (419, 504); Relationships (1034, 1242)]. He was present during the interrogation of primary suspect Arthur Leigh Allen and was mentioned by name in a Zodiac letter [Data: Entities (419)]. However, this mention later led to false accusations that Toschi himself had written the 1978 Zodiac letter [Data: Entities (419, 656); Relationships (1674)]. Although he was ultimately cleared of these charges, the controversy significantly impacted his career, leading to his political destruction, removal from Homicide, and eventual retirement from the SFPD in 1989 [Data: Entities (419, 579); Relationships (1430)]. His wife was also involved, speaking with Graysmith from their kitchen [Data: Entities (580); Relationships (1431)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Zodiac Letters as a Primary Communication and Investigative Tool\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The Zodiac Killer utilized various letters as a primary means of communication with the public and authorities, often containing clues, threats, or personal messages. One such letter, which arrived on December 20th, contained messages and clues, notably mentioning Toschi [Data: Entities (331, 549); Relationships (1348, 794)]. Another Zodiac letter was addressed to Melvin Belli, where the killer reportedly asked for help and mentioned potential future victims [Data: Entities (331)]. A new Zodiac letter was spotted by Parker and announced via police radio, indicating ongoing communication from the killer [Data: Entities (556, 566, 331); Relationships (1405, 1422)]. The content and timing of these letters varied significantly, serving as crucial elements in the investigation [Data: Entities (331)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"The Homicide Unit Overwhelmed by the Zodiac Case\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The Homicide Unit, located within the Hall of Justice and part of the San Francisco Police Department, was primarily responsible for investigating murders, most notably the Zodiac killer case [Data: Entities (222, 559); Relationships (1411)]. Due to the significant volume of calls and tips related to the Zodiac case, the unit became known as \\\"Zodiac Central\\\" [Data: Entities (222, 235); Relationships (526)]. Detectives like Toschi and Armstrong were assigned to this unit, actively working on investigations, making phone calls, and awaiting results, pursuing all leads and managing open cases [Data: Entities (222, 419, 514, 534, 535); Relationships (1294, 1295)]. Captain Martin Lee oversaw the Homicide Unit's investigation [Data: Entities (226); Relationships (525)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"The Paul Stine Murder at Washington and Cherry as a Key Crime Scene\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The intersection of Washington and Cherry in Pacific Heights, San Francisco, was identified as the scene of a significant crime: the shooting death of cab driver Paul Stine, attributed to the Zodiac killer [Data: Entities (154, 160, 525, 522); Relationships (348, 1267, 1269)]. This location was a crucial site, regularly visited by detectives Toschi and Armstrong, who sat vigil there, and also by Robert Graysmith [Data: Entities (160, 419, 514)]. The incident was reported to the San Francisco Police by a teenage girl caller, who described a fight and robbery in a cab [Data: Entities (163, 162); Relationships (352, 354)]. Emergency services, including an ambulance driven by Dousette and a Fire Department vehicle, were present at the crime scene, along with a Crime Lab Van [Data: Entities (183, 181, 182, 173); Relationships (373, 371, 372, 379)]. The death occurred eight years prior to October 11, 1977 [Data: Entities (521, 525); Relationships (1268)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"The Influence of 'Dirty Harry' on Public Perception and the Investigation\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The movie 'Dirty Harry,' featuring a fictional killer named Scorpio and protagonist Harry Callahan, was screened for San Francisco's elite, including Toschi and his wife, Robert Graysmith, Melanie, and the Police Commissioner [Data: Entities (493, 498, 505, 497)]. The film, set in San Francisco, depicted a killer sending letters and taking children hostage from Fourth Street School, mirroring aspects of the real-life Zodiac case [Data: Entities (493, 494, 495); Relationships (1220, 1230)]. The Mayor of San Francisco, portrayed by John Vernon, was depicted reading a killer's letter projected onto a screen in his office within the movie [Data: Entities (487, 488); Relationships (1203, 1205)]. The Police Commissioner even spoke to Dave Toschi, commenting on the movie's resolution of 'your case,' highlighting the public's perception and the blurred lines between fiction and the ongoing investigation [Data: Entities (497); Relationships (1237)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \